# ─── Securing AI, environment ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every variable is OPTIONAL. With nothing set, the app runs in deterministic
# stub mode: all scenarios, scoring and UI are fully functional, only the
# free-form model output is replaced with a stub message.


# ─── Model provider ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Enables real AI replies in Dojo 2 and 3, the benign/probing path in Dojo 1,
# and the quiz question generator. Dojo 1 attack outcomes stay deterministic
# either way and never reach a provider.
#
# Read server-side only, in lib/model-client.ts. It is never sent to the
# browser and there is no NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable anywhere in this project.
#
# IMPORTANT: setting this makes three unauthenticated endpoints spend real
# money, because the app has no accounts. Before setting it in a public
# deployment, set a monthly budget limit on the provider account itself
# (platform.openai.com, Settings, Limits). That cap is the only hard ceiling.
# The in-app controls below are a brake, not a guarantee.
OPENAI_API_KEY=


# ─── Shared daily request budget ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Total model-bound requests allowed per UTC day across all callers, enforced
# in lib/api-guard.ts. A chat turn costs 1 unit; generating questions costs 4,
# since it is a much larger call. When the budget is spent the AI features
# return 503 with an explanation and the rest of the app keeps working.
#
# Default is 2000. As a rough guide, a chat turn on gpt-4o-mini is on the order
# of $0.001, so 2000 requests is a couple of dollars per day per instance.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION: this counter, like the per-IP rate limiter, lives in memory
# on each serverless instance. Vercel runs several instances and recycles them,
# so the effective ceiling is this number multiplied by the number of warm
# instances, and a cold start resets it. Lower it if you want more headroom,
# and rely on the provider-side cap for the real limit.
DAILY_MODEL_REQUEST_LIMIT=2000
